\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

Crossing rate reverses the central finding via shallow-layer compression (\cref{app:cosine_volatility,tab:r18b_full_layer}): its shallow preference is an artifact of cosine compression inflating threshold crossings, while threshold-based deep preference reflects genuine separability~\citep{tenney2019bert}; random-label controls confirm this distinction (\cref{app:random_cp}). C1 generalizes across all four models (cross-dataset: \cref{app:cosine_volatility}; split-half: \cref{app:split_half}); C2 is sample-dependent (7--8B cross-validated, 32B descriptive). Annotation noise (${<}2.5$pp BA change; \cref{tab:cp_perturbation}) does not threaten C1. The encoder-quality gradient narrows the HS advantage (384d to 1024d; \cref{app:position_residual}). Type~I risk from ${\sim}$2{,}240 configurations is bounded by the two-stage design and large effect sizes ($d$ 0.58--2.37; \cref{app:effective_tests}). \textbf{Practical mitigation.} A multi-metric audit (\cref{app:deployment_operating_points}) flags divergent conclusions when ${\geq}3$ metrics differ by ${>}20\%$ of depth. A three-layer ensemble eliminates pre-selection ($p \leq 0.002$; \cref{tab:ensemble}) but lacks cross-validation; this is diagnostic guidance, not a validated method.\looseness=-1

